   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the election law, in relation to signatures for certain party\npositions; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration\nthereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S2699",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S2699-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §6-136, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2699\n\nSPONSOR: BAILEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to signatures for certain\nparty positions; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon\nexpiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo reduce barriers for candidates petitioning to get on the ballot.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill adds a new subdivision four to § 6-136 of the\nelection law to reduce the petition signature requirement on party posi-\ntions for ward, town, city, or county committees to 3 percent of\nenrolled voters of the party residing within the election district.\n\nSection 2 of the bill is the effective date and provides that the law\nwill expire on December 31, 2020.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2009, Congress passed the Military and Overseas Voters Act (MOVE),\nwhich required a number of reforms to ensure military, federal workers,\nand overseas voters had the opportunity to cast their ballot in the\ngeneral election in a timely manner. In 2012, US District Judge Gary\nSharpe ruled that New York State's September primary was in violation of\nthe MOVE Act and subsequently ordered the federal primary to be moved to\nthe 4th Tuesday in June, leaving it to the legislature to determine when\nto hold state and local primaries. This left New York with two separate\nprimaries: one for federal offices and one for state and local offices.\n\nIn 2019, after 8 years of impasse the legislature has passed legislation\nreunifying state, local, and federal (non-presidential) primary\nelections to be held on the 4th Tuesday in June, effective immediately.\n\nHowever, because of the change in the political calendar necessary to\nmeet the requirements for a June primary, candidates for party positions\nwill have to circulate designating petitions primarily during the last\nweeks of winter to obtain enough signatures to appear oh the ballot.\nThis change in the calendar necessitates a two year modification to\nsection 6-136 of the election law to allow the impact of the new poli-\ntical calendar to be evaluated.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT ON THE STATE:\n\nNone.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately, except that it shall expire on\nDecember 31, 2020, and thereafter be deemed repealed.\n",
